# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF, EVERYWHERE, IN THE REPOSITORY AND IN THE WORKING TREE.
#
# The repository already stored LF in every one of its files; what varied was the
# CHECKOUT, because `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows machine rewrites a file to
# CRLF while every tool here — gofmt, the emitters, the compiler's own writers —
# produces LF. The result was a working tree of mixed endings: 242 of the tracked
# files were CRLF and the rest LF, which made `gofmt -l` list files nobody had
# touched and made `git add` warn on files that were already correct.
#
# `eol=lf` decides it for every machine rather than per developer's config.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two Windows scripts a person runs by hand, where CRLF is the platform's own
# convention. `cmd` tolerates LF today; a label or a `goto` split across a line
# ending is the classic way that stops being true.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The emission baseline is compared BYTE FOR BYTE (gauntlet/check/README.md), so git must
# never convert its line endings on checkout: a Windows checkout would otherwise turn every
# LF into CRLF and report every emitted file as changed. These come AFTER the rule above,
# because the last matching line wins.
gauntlet/check/testdata/emitted/** -text
gauntlet/check/outcomes.txt -text
